#!/usr/bin/env bash

# Check for staged changes
diff=$(git diff --cached | head -n 10)
if [ -z "$diff" ]; then
  echo "No changes in staging. Add changes first."
  exit 1
fi

COMMITS_TO_SUGGEST=5 # Required quantity per final example

pnpm dlx @anthropic-ai/claude-code -p "
You are an expert at writing Git commits. Your job is to write a short clear commit message that summarizes the changes.

General Git style rules:
- Try to limit the subject line to 70 characters
- Never exceed 72 characters in the subject
- Capitalize the subject line
- Do not end the subject line with any punctuation
- Use the imperative mood in the subject line
- Explain 'what' and 'why', not 'how'

Only return the commit message(s) in your response. Do not include any additional meta-commentary.
Generate $COMMITS_TO_SUGGEST different commit suggestions.

IMPORTANT: Do not wrap your response in markdown code blocks. Do not use \`\`\` in your response at all.
Just list each commit message directly, one per line.

Here is an some info on current state of the repository:

<git_status>
$(git status)
</git_status>

<git_log>
$(git log --oneline -10)
</git_log>

Here is the diff:

<git_diff>
$(git diff --staged)
</git_diff>

If you need more context, you can also look at the staged files by running git commands yourself.
"
